# The computer
// How the computer picks its move, when you play against it:  every move it can make, each given points.
// - a jump scores most:  the rules make it jump when it can, so this picks between jumps
// - then a move that crowns a king
// - a move onto a square where no piece of yours can jump it scores, and one where you can loses
// It looks one move ahead, no more:  it never sets a trap, and you can beat it.

a "move" is a thing
	-- One move the side to play could make:  a piece, the square it would go to, and its points
	its "piece" is a piece
	its "square" is a square
	its "points" is a number

a square
	it "is next to (a square)" if
		-- Whether it touches the square at a corner:  one row and one column away
		return the absolute value of (its row - the row of the square) is 1 and the absolute value of (its column - the column of the square) is 1

a piece
	it "would be safe on (a square)" if
		-- Whether no enemy piece could jump it there:  one next to the square, with the square beyond it free
		-- inside the loop `its` is the square beside:  so its side and where it stands are named first
		set board to the board of its square
		set own side to its side
		set left to its square
		set threats to 0
		for beside in board where it is next to the square
			set enemy to the piece of beside
			set landing to the square at the row of the square * 2 - the row of beside and the column of the square * 2 - the column of beside on board
			if enemy exists and landing exists and the side of enemy is not own side
				-- it lands where this piece left, or on an empty square;  a man jumps only forward
				set heading to (the row of landing - the row of beside) * the forward of enemy
				if (landing is left or landing is empty) and (enemy is a king or heading > 0)
					set threats to threats + 1
		return threats is 0

to "the points for (a move)"
	-- What a move is worth to the computer
	set mover to the piece of the move
	set target to the square of the move
	set score to 0
	if mover can jump to target
		set score to score + 10
	if mover is not a king and the row of target is the far row of mover
		set score to score + 5
	if mover would be safe on target
		set score to score + 2
	otherwise
		set score to score - 4
	return score

a board
	its "moves" is
		-- Every move the side whose turn it is can make, with its points
		set found to a new list of moves
		for start in it where its piece exists and the side of its piece is the turn of the board
			set mover to the piece of start
			for target in the board where mover can go to it
				set move to a new move with piece = mover, square = target
				set the points of move to the points for move
				add move to found
		return found

to "play for the computer on (a board)"
	-- The computer's move:  the most points, picked at random among the best
	set choices to the moves of the board
	if choices is empty
		return
	set best to the last move in (choices sorted by its points)
	set chosen to a random move in choices where its points is the points of best
	move the piece of chosen to the square of chosen

to test the computer jumps
	set board to a new board
	reset board
	set the turn of board to red
	put a "red" "man" at 3 and 4 on board
	put a "black" "man" at 4 and 5 on board
	put a "black" "man" at 7 and 2 on board
	play for the computer on board
	the square at 4 and 5 on board is empty should be yes
	the piece of (the square at 5 and 6 on board) exists should be yes

to test the computer keeps its pieces safe
	set board to a new board
	reset board
	set the turn of board to red
	put a "red" "man" at 3 and 4 on board
	put a "black" "man" at 6 and 7 on board
	-- going to 4 and 5 walks into a jump from 5 and 6 later;  4 and 3 is safe now
	set man to the piece of (the square at 3 and 4 on board)
	man would be safe on (the square at 4 and 5 on board) should be yes
	put a "black" "man" at 5 and 6 on board
	man would be safe on (the square at 4 and 5 on board) should be no
	man would be safe on (the square at 4 and 3 on board) should be yes
	play for the computer on board
	the piece of (the square at 4 and 3 on board) exists should be yes
